TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2) – Space Mission Design","overview":"Students design a preliminary space mission benefiting Australia, covering the space segment: mission purpose, spacecraft count, users, launch site and vehicle, orbit and orbital elements, manoeuvres, ground control and communications, mission duration and end-of-life. Design may use STK, PIGI or approved software, supplemented by hand calculations, and is reported as a concise technical report with supporting screenshots and software files."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale - Include the following required information: • What is the spacecraft's primary mission? • Why is the mission needed? • How many spacecraft are used and why? • Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design - Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate - Include relevant screenshots and software files (in the main body of the report and/or appendix) - Include the following information: • Where it will launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? • What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? • Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? • Which ground control facilities will you use? • How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? • How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (eg. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Executive Summary"},{"id":"R2","met":"partial","where":"Mission Design"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"Executive Summary"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R5","met":"partial","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R6","met":"partial","where":"Mission Design - Launch"},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R8","met":"partial","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R10","met":"partial","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R11","met":"partial","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R12","met":"partial","where":"Image 1-5"},{"id":"R13","met":"yes","where":"Contents / Bibliography"},{"id":"R14","met":"no","where":"Image 1-5 and tables"},{"id":"R15","met":"no","where":"Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Mission Design - Launch","issue":"Piggyback rideshare is described, yet the Executive Summary says 'direct launch' and the Orbit section says the satellite 'can be launched directly'. Vega E is unbuilt, with a first flight of 2024 that is unsupported."},{"where":"Orbit - COE table","issue":"RAAN 27.33° is stated to indicate the launch facility location. Kourou is near 53°W, so this is incorrect, and the epoch is not given."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"Altitude reasoning contradicts itself: resolution must be high enough, yet high altitude 'yields low resolution images which is also desirable'. No swath width or GSD calculation is shown."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"Perturbation and delta-v calculations are claimed in the Executive Summary but not shown. Orbit insertion manoeuvre is omitted."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"Tasmania (~42°S) is described as a near-pole facility choice. Access table lists a duplicate row 15 identical to row 14, and a stray '----' row."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"Claim that the descending side is 'constantly in sunlight' is wrong. Eclipse depends on beta angle, and local time of the node is not stated."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"Passive uncontrolled decay from 600 km takes decades to centuries, failing the 25-year guideline. The text says the drag sail is the 'most promising alternative' but leaves the EOL decision unresolved and contains a truncated sentence."},{"where":"Citations","issue":"Tables cite [49]-[74] and Falcon table cites [10], none of which match the 10-entry bibliography. [10] is also used for the drag-sail source. Wikipedia [6] is used, which is not accepted."}],"overall":"Solid, mostly complete space-segment design with STK evidence, but significant technical contradictions (launch vehicle, RAAN, altitude trade-off, EOL compliance) and unsupported claims. Figures and tables are unlabelled and citations are inconsistent, with Wikipedia used. Weighted score approximately 49/100.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Adequate","mark":6.0,"scale":10.0,"points":6.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Primary mission (reef bleaching monitoring with fluid lensing), rationale and single-spacecraft choice are clearly argued and cited. Users are vague: customers are named but the operator is not identified beyond 'Australia ground facilities'. Wikipedia is cited as a source, and several claims lack support.","evidence":[{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The proposed satellite would likely be used by governmental and science agencies (e.g. CSIRO, BOM) for research and/or education."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Thus, only one satellite is required."}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":30.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"Good coverage of all space-segment items with STK access data, ground tracks and a Keplerian element table. However, the launch choice is contradictory (piggyback versus direct launch, unbuilt Vega E). RAAN is wrongly tied to the launch site. The altitude logic is inconsistent, no swath or delta-v calculations are shown, and the EOL strategy is unresolved. Access table has a duplicated row. Citations to tables are not in the bibliography.","evidence":[{"where":"Mission Design - Launch","quote":"The proposed satellite will thus use the Vega E rocket as a launch vehicle"},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"The initial value indicates the location of the launch facility."},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"A very high altitude would yield low resolution images which is also desirable."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"access frequency is maximised by selecting a ground facility close to the poles"},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"where the satellite is constantly in sunlight"},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"However, the ZYZZsat is without thrust capability and so must use a"}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":5.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The conclusion recaps the segments and notes launch-vehicle risk with a fallback option, which is a reasonable conclusion. It is largely a restatement, however, and does not weigh the unresolved issues (EOL compliance, altitude trade-off, launch contradictions) against the evidence.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"A polar sun-synchronous orbit allows for consistent illumination of the oceans while maintaining a constant sun angle."},{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"Alternatives include the earlier Vega C launcher."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"Clear section structure, contents list and headings. Writing has several grammar and typo errors ('a Earth', 'purposed', 'complied'). Figures and tables lack numbers and titles, and in-text citations such as [49]-[74] do not match the 10-item bibliography. Stray characters appear in references.","evidence":[{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"construction of a Earth observation satellite"},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"enough for the purposed of this satellite"},{"where":"Image 1 and Image 2","quote":"STK 3D view of orbit: 12 hours apart"},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_in_spaceflight"}]}]}
